# Line endings are part of the CONTRACT for anything that runs inside a Linux
# container or is exec'd by a kernel, not a style preference.
#
# Windows checkouts default to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF in
# the working tree. Several of these files are compiled into the outpost binary
# with go:embed, so a CRLF working tree produces a binary carrying CRLF scripts
# — and `go:embed` is byte-exact, so nothing downstream corrects it.
#
# The failure that motivated this is worth recording because it is invisible:
# the container runtime image built on a Windows host had an /entrypoint.sh whose
# shebang read `#!/bin/sh\r`. The kernel then looks for an interpreter literally
# named "/bin/sh\r", does not find it, and reports:
#
#     exec container process (missing dynamic library?) `/entrypoint.sh`:
#     No such file or directory
#
# which points at the script rather than at its interpreter and reads like a
# missing file or a broken image. The file was present and executable; it was
# 447 bytes larger than the same file on macOS — exactly one CR per its 447
# lines. The DKS agent node on that host stayed NotReady with no other symptom.
#
# Default: let git normalize text in the repository, and keep the working tree
# LF for everything that must be read by a Unix kernel or shell.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the files where a CRLF would be fatal rather than merely untidy.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Containerfile   text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts genuinely want CRLF.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binary, never touched.
*.png           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary
*.tar           binary
